# Explain rnc

Give the developer a complete overview of rnc's AI infrastructure — what exists, how it works, and how to use it day to day.

## Step 1 — Read the source files

Read these files before explaining anything:

- `CLAUDE.md`
- `cortex/README.md`
- `cortex/CHANGELOG.md`

## Step 2 — Deliver the explanation

Structure the explanation as follows. Be concise — one clear paragraph or table per section. No padding.

---

### What rnc is

What the monorepo contains, what each sub-project does, and the core workflow principle (monorepo-always — always work from root, delete unused modules, rename folders for each project).

### How the AI tooling is structured

Explain the two layers:

- **Rules** (`.claude/rules/` and `.cursor/rules/`) — activate automatically by file path via glob patterns. No manual loading during implementation.
- **Context** (`CLAUDE.md` and `project-context.mdc`) — always loaded, gives the AI project awareness from the first message.

Explain the planning gap and how `/battoni-start-session` solves it.

### Available commands

A table of every command, what it does, and when to use it:

| Command | When to use |
| --- | --- |
| `/battoni-setup-project` | Once, right after cloning and renaming folders for a new project |
| `/battoni-start-session` | Every session — loads the right rules before planning begins |
| `/battoni-review-vue-conventions` | Before opening a PR that touches app.rnc — checks Vue/TS convention violations |
| `/battoni-review-design-conventions` | Before opening a PR that touches app.rnc — checks design system violations (colors, typography, spacing, radius, buttons) |
| `/battoni-review-arcus-code` | Before opening a PR that touches api.rnc |
| `/battoni-generate-component-rules` | When adding a new component folder — generates a `RULES.md` with folder-specific AI instructions |
| `/battoni-create-pr` | When ready to open a PR — generates title, description, and pre-merge checklist |
| `/battoni-generate-api-rules` | When adding an api.rnc unit — generates its `RULES.md` + `CHANGELOG.md` |
| `/battoni-rules-sync` | Before a PR — refreshes the `RULES.md` of every unit the branch changed and bumps its version (`--check` only reports) |
| `/battoni-pre-commit` | After your own review, before committing — runs `make pre-commit` and the convention reviews; commits nothing |
| `/battoni-commit` | Commits the changes, grouped by concern |
| `/battoni-publish-pr` | Pushes the branch and opens a draft PR from `/battoni-create-pr`'s output |
| `/battoni-grill-me` | Before a task — a tech-lead interrogation that ends in an approved plan |
| `/battoni-improve` | When the AI got something wrong — turns it into a rule change and a PR |
| `/battoni-agent-handoff` | When another agent must resume the work — writes a zero-context handoff |
| `/battoni-explain-rnc` | This command — explains everything to a new developer |

### Rules in detail

Which rules exist, what they cover, and when each one activates. Include the shared conventions rule (always loaded).

### Skills, agents, and hooks

- **Skills** — what each skill covers and when it auto-loads
- **Agents** — what `code-reviewer` and `security-auditor` do and how to invoke them
- **Hooks** — what runs automatically after file edits (Pint for PHP, ESLint for Vue/TS) and what the bash guard blocks

### Starting a new project

The exact steps from clone to first session:

```
1. Clone rnc
2. Delete unused modules
3. Rename folders (e.g. api.rnc → api.myproject, app.rnc → app.myproject)
4. Run /battoni-setup-project
5. Run /battoni-start-session
```

### Day-to-day workflow

```
Start of session:     /battoni-start-session
During development:   rules load automatically as you edit files
Before a PR:          /battoni-pre-commit (runs /battoni-review-vue-conventions, /battoni-review-design-conventions
                      and /battoni-review-arcus-code where the repo has an app or an API)
Opening a PR:         /battoni-create-pr
```

### How to add or update a rule

Edit only the canonical file in `.claude/rules/` (or `.claude/commands/`), then run `make sync-mirror` to regenerate its `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` mirror — never edit the mirror by hand. A repo whose Makefile has no `sync-mirror` (a tooling-only copy) edits the `.mdc` twin too, with a body identical to the `.claude` source. `make sync-mirror-check` (part of `make pre-commit`) and the CI mirror job fail when the two bodies differ. Regenerate the handbook pages with `make docs-gen`, and add an entry to `cortex/CHANGELOG.md`.

**Retired:** "No syncing needed — there is only one copy of each rule." There are two, and `cortex/sync/cursor_mirror.py` is what keeps them one.

